A larger Moth website is a tree of pages and shared code. Each page has an
`@entry.moth` file. Reusable navigation and layout helpers live in a scoped
support package.

### One site, several pages

[codeblock, $code "text":
    moth-site/
      config.moth
      @entry.moth
      components/
        @package.moth
        navigation.moth
        sidebar.moth
        sidebar.css
      docs/
        @entry.moth
        functions/
          @entry.moth
          introduction.mtf
]

Each directory containing `@entry.moth` produces a page. The HTML builder
derives the route from the directory, so `docs/functions/@entry.moth` produces
`docs/functions/index.html`.

`config.moth` holds optional build settings. It never becomes a page or joins
the module graph.

The `components/` directory holds an `@package.moth` root, which makes
`@components` a scoped support package. Pages import its exported declarations
without copying source or running the package as a page.

### A page that composes content

[codeblock, $code "moth":
    -- docs/functions/@entry.moth

    @components navbar, sidebar
    @introduction content as introduction

    [$in title:Functions]
    [$in description:Functions, parameters and returns.]

    [navbar]
    [sidebar]

    [$md:
        # Functions

        Functions declare their parameters and results.
    ]

    [introduction]
]

The page mixes authored Markdown with imported templates. A `.mtf` asset
supplies a compile-time `content` String, so a longer chapter can stay in
`introduction.mtf` instead of making the entry file unwieldy.

Direct entry templates fold at compile time. A direct `["~[...]"]` fragment
asks for runtime evaluation instead. A template stored in a binding stays an
ordinary String value, and it becomes page output only when something inserts
it.

### Publish a small helper surface

The support package facade exposes only what pages should use:

[codeblock, $code "moth":
    -- components/@package.moth

    $export
    @navigation navbar

    $export
    @sidebar sidebar
]

Each `$export` publishes one declaration or one selected re-export. Private
helpers stay inside the package.

### Where shared code goes

Templates and constants used by several pages belong in the support package.
Functions that only the package calls belong in ordinary `.moth` files beside
it.

`@mod.moth` publishes a normal API to the parent module, while `@package.moth`
publishes a scoped support API. Both facades use `$export`, and neither can
expose entry work.

Start with this shape. Add a module only when it marks a real visibility or
ownership boundary, not for every template or stylesheet.
